\title{Infinitesimal Point Masses on the Finite--Cofinite Algebra}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We construct a normalized surreal-valued probability on the finite--cofinite algebra of any infinite sample space. The construction begins with the Conway cut
\[
\varepsilon=\left\{0\ \middle|\ 1,2^{-1},2^{-2},\ldots\right\},
\]
which is strictly positive and smaller than $2^{-n}$ for every $n\in\N$. In particular, every finite multiple $n\varepsilon$ is less than one. For an event $A$ that is finite or cofinite, define $P(A)=|A|\varepsilon$ in the finite case and $P(A)=1-|A^c|\varepsilon$ in the cofinite case. We prove that this definition is unambiguous on an infinite space, nonnegative, normalized, and additive on disjoint pairs. Every singleton consequently has the same strictly positive infinitesimal probability. Specializing to $[0,1]$ yields an explicit non-Archimedean probability model with positive point masses and total mass one. The result concerns finite additivity on the finite--cofinite algebra; it makes no claim of countable additivity on a power set or Borel $\sigma$-algebra. We also give an exact symbolic algorithm for calculations, discuss the classical shadow, and identify the structures required for extensions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A familiar feature of continuous probability is that every singleton has probability zero. Under uniform probability on $[0,1]$, one has $P(\{x\})=0$ for every $x$, while $P([0,1])=1$. There is no contradiction: countable additivity does not add an uncountable collection of singleton masses, and ordinary length assigns zero to points.

Nevertheless, it is natural to ask whether a point can receive a \emph{positive infinitesimal} probability: a value greater than zero but smaller than every ordinary positive scale relevant to the construction. The real numbers cannot provide it. Their Archimedean property says that for every real $r>0$, some natural multiple $nr$ exceeds one. A genuinely infinitesimal point mass therefore requires a non-Archimedean ordered field or ring.

Conway's surreal numbers provide a direct setting. They contain the real numbers and admit numbers specified recursively by left and right options. We use the cut with left option zero and right options $1,1/2,1/4,\ldots$. This gives a positive surreal $\varepsilon$ below every reciprocal power of two.

The second design choice is the event domain. Assigning a common infinitesimal to every point does not determine the mass of arbitrary infinite subsets. We work with the finite--cofinite algebra: events are exactly finite sets and sets with finite complement. Its geometry is simple enough that pointwise pricing determines every event, yet rich enough to include all singletons, their finite unions, complements, and the whole space.

The theory separates the value domain (the surreal numbers), the event algebra (finite and cofinite subsets), and the additivity principle (finite additivity). Our main theorem gives finite events mass $|A|\varepsilon$ and cofinite events mass $1-|A^c|\varepsilon$. This is normalized, nonnegative, finitely additive, and gives every singleton mass $\varepsilon$. The restrictions are essential: we do not assign masses to all subsets or Borel sets, nor do we define infinite sums of surreal values.

\section{Surreal preliminaries}

A surreal number may be represented by a cut $\{L\mid R\}$, where every member of $L$ is less than every member of $R$. The cut denotes the simplest surreal above every left option and below every right option. Only these order consequences are needed here.

\begin{definition}[Dyadic surreal infinitesimal]
Define
\[
\varepsilon=\left\{0\ \middle|\ 1,\frac12,\frac14,\frac18,\ldots\right\}
=\left\{0\ \middle|\ 2^{-n}:n\in\N\right\}.
\]
Here $\N$ contains zero. The cut is valid because $0<2^{-n}$ for every $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity and dyadic domination]
For every $n\in\N$,
\[
0<\varepsilon<2^{-n}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A number represented by a valid cut lies strictly above each left option and strictly below each right option. Zero is the sole left option, and every $2^{-n}$ is a right option.
\end{proof}

No positive real has this property: for every real $r>0$, repeated halving eventually gives $2^{-n}<r$.

\begin{lemma}[Finite-multiple bound]
For every $n\in\N$, one has $n\varepsilon<1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The elementary inequality $n\le 2^n$ holds for every natural number. Since $\varepsilon>0$ and $\varepsilon<2^{-n}$,
\[
n\varepsilon\le 2^n\varepsilon<2^n2^{-n}=1.
\]
For $n=0$, the same chain reduces to $0<1$.
\end{proof}

Thus both $n\varepsilon$ and $1-n\varepsilon$ are nonnegative for every finite $n$, with the latter strictly positive.

\section{The finite--cofinite event algebra}

Let $X$ be an infinite set, and write $A^c=X\setminus A$.

\begin{definition}[Admissible event]
A subset $A\subseteq X$ is admissible if either $A$ is finite or $A^c$ is finite. In the latter case $A$ is \emph{cofinite}. Set
\[
\mathcal F_X=\{A\subseteq X:A\text{ is finite or }A^c\text{ is finite}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Boolean closure]
The collection $\mathcal F_X$ contains $\varnothing$ and $X$ and is closed under complements and finite unions. Hence it is a Boolean algebra of subsets of $X$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The empty set is finite, and $X$ is cofinite because $X^c=\varnothing$. Complementation exchanges ``finite'' and ``cofinite.'' If both sets are finite, their union is finite. If one is cofinite, the complement of the union is an intersection contained in its finite complement, hence is finite. Intersections follow from De Morgan's laws.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Disjoint alternatives]
No event in $\mathcal F_X$ is both finite and cofinite.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $A$ and $A^c$ were finite, then $X=A\cup A^c$ would be finite, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{The probability construction}

\begin{definition}[Surreal finite--cofinite probability]
For $A\in\mathcal F_X$, define
\[
P(A)=
\begin{cases}
|A|\varepsilon,&A\text{ is finite},\\
1-|A^c|\varepsilon,&A\text{ is cofinite}.
\end{cases}
\]
The preceding lemma makes this definition unambiguous.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Normalization]
$P(X)=1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The complement of $X$ is empty, so $P(X)=1-|\varnothing|\varepsilon=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Uniform infinitesimal singleton mass]
For every $x\in X$,
\[
P(\{x\})=\varepsilon,
\qquad 0<P(\{x\})<2^{-n}\quad(n\in\N).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A singleton has cardinality one, so its mass is $\varepsilon$. The inequalities are the positivity and dyadic domination lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Nonnegativity and bounds]
Every admissible event satisfies $0\le P(A)\le1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
If $A$ is finite, then $P(A)=|A|\varepsilon\ge0$ and the finite-multiple bound gives $P(A)<1$. If $A$ is cofinite, put $n=|A^c|$. Then $P(A)=1-n\varepsilon>0$, while $n\varepsilon\ge0$ gives $P(A)\le1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite additivity]
If $A,B\in\mathcal F_X$ and $A\cap B=\varnothing$, then
\[
P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If both events are finite, disjointness gives $|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|$, so
\[
P(A\cup B)=(|A|+|B|)\varepsilon=P(A)+P(B).
\]
Suppose $A$ is finite and $B$ is cofinite. Disjointness gives $A\subseteq B^c$ and the disjoint decomposition
\[
B^c=A\mathbin{\dot\cup}(A^c\cap B^c)
=A\mathbin{\dot\cup}(A\cup B)^c.
\]
Thus $|B^c|=|A|+|(A\cup B)^c|$. If $r=|(A\cup B)^c|$, then
\[
\begin{aligned}
P(A)+P(B)
&=|A|\varepsilon+1-|B^c|\varepsilon\\
&=|A|\varepsilon+1-(|A|+r)\varepsilon\\
&=1-r\varepsilon=P(A\cup B).
\end{aligned}
\]
The case with $A$ cofinite and $B$ finite is symmetric. Two cofinite events cannot be disjoint: otherwise De Morgan's law would give $A^c\cup B^c=X$, expressing the infinite set $X$ as a finite union of finite sets.
\end{proof}

Induction extends the theorem to every finite pairwise-disjoint family $A_1,\ldots,A_k$:
\[
P\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^k A_i\right)=\sum_{i=1}^kP(A_i).
\]

\begin{corollary}[Complement law]
For every $A\in\mathcal F_X$, $P(A^c)=1-P(A)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply finite additivity to the disjoint union $A\cup A^c=X$ and use normalization.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monotonicity]
If $A,B\in\mathcal F_X$ and $A\subseteq B$, then $P(A)\le P(B)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The difference $B\setminus A$ is admissible, and $B=A\mathbin{\dot\cup}(B\setminus A)$. Finite additivity and nonnegativity give $P(B)=P(A)+P(B\setminus A)\ge P(A)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Inclusion--exclusion]
For all $A,B\in\mathcal F_X$,
\[
P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cap B).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Decompose $A\cup B$ into the pairwise-disjoint events $A\setminus B$, $B\setminus A$, and $A\cap B$. Apply finite additivity to this decomposition and to $A$ and $B$, then cancel common terms.
\end{proof}

\section{The unit interval}

\begin{theorem}[Infinitesimal probability on the unit interval]
Let $X=[0,1]$ and let $\mathcal F_X$ be its finite--cofinite event algebra. There is a function $P:\mathcal F_X\to\No$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $P(X)=1$;
\item $P(A)\ge0$ for every $A\in\mathcal F_X$;
\item for every $x\in[0,1]$, $P(\{x\})=\varepsilon>0$ and $P(\{x\})<2^{-n}$ for all $n\in\N$;
\item if $A,B\in\mathcal F_X$ are disjoint, then $P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)$.
\end{enumerate}
Here $\No$ denotes the surreal numbers.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The interval is infinite; for example, it contains the distinct sequence $0,1/2,1/3,\ldots$. Apply the preceding construction and results.
\end{proof}

A typical interval such as $[0,1/2]$ is not admissible because it and its complement relative to $[0,1]$ are both infinite. The construction supplements rather than reproduces length-based probability.

\section{Exact symbolic computation}

Every probability has the form $n\varepsilon$ or $1-n\varepsilon$. Encode the affine expression $a+b\varepsilon$ by $(a,b)$. Then addition is componentwise:
\[
(a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d).
\]

\begin{algorithm}[Event-mass evaluation]
Given an event tagged as finite with cardinality $n$, return $(0,n)$. Given one tagged as cofinite with complement cardinality $n$, return $(1,-n)$.
\end{algorithm}

Once the cardinality is supplied, time and memory use are $O(1)$. For an explicit finite set, hash-based deduplication and counting take expected $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ memory.

\begin{algorithm}[Disjoint-union audit]
For two explicit finite or cofinite events, test structural disjointness, calculate both input masses and the union mass, add affine pairs componentwise, and compare. Two finite events use ordinary set disjointness. A finite event $F$ and cofinite event $X\setminus M$ are disjoint exactly when $F\subseteq M$; their union misses $M\setminus F$. Two cofinite events over an infinite universe are never disjoint.
\end{algorithm}

With hash sets, the audit takes expected $O(|F|+|M|)$ time and comparable memory. If a cofinite event omits five points and a disjoint finite event restores two of them, then
\[
(1-5\varepsilon)+2\varepsilon=1-3\varepsilon,
\]
which is the mass of the union.

Replacing $\varepsilon$ by a floating-point proxy may illustrate coefficients, but no fixed positive real proxy satisfies $n\varepsilon<1$ for every natural $n$. The affine representation preserves the relevant distinction exactly.

\section{Interpretation and applications}

The construction shows that positive singleton masses, unit total mass, and finite additivity can coexist. The apparent tension comes from an Archimedean inference: sufficiently many copies of a positive real exceed one. The surreal $\varepsilon$ invalidates precisely that inference while retaining ordered arithmetic.

The probability is invariant under every permutation $\pi:X\to X$. Bijections preserve finite cardinalities and complement cardinalities, so $P(\pi(A))=P(A)$. No point is privileged.

The affine correction also distinguishes events that ordinary zero--one finite--cofinite probability identifies. Missing one point has mass $1-\varepsilon$; missing a thousand has mass $1-1000\varepsilon$. Both have ordinary shadow one, but their infinitesimal corrections retain finite-exception information. Likewise, finite events of different cardinality have distinct surreal masses although all have ordinary shadow zero.

This can support idealized symmetry models, perturbative bookkeeping, and lexicographic comparisons of events. The theorem supplies algebraic probability identities, not yet a complete theory of expectation, conditioning, or stochastic processes.

A related hyperfinite picture uses an infinite natural number $N$ and a grid of $N$ equally weighted atoms of mass $1/N$. The present construction instead uses a specific surreal cut and no chosen infinite grid cardinality. A precise comparison requires a common value framework and a standard-part operation.

\section{Scope and limitations}

The theorem proves finite, not countable, additivity. A countably additive law requires a meaning for
\[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P(A_k)
\]
in the surreal codomain. Infinite sums are not automatically supplied by ordered-field operations; admissibility and convergence must be specified. Moreover, ordinary $\sigma$-additivity concerns countable unions, not an uncountable sum over all points of $[0,1]$.

The finite--cofinite algebra omits most geometrically interesting subsets of the interval. Equal point masses do not determine interval masses. Enlarging the domain requires further principles, perhaps compatibility with length, symmetry, or a standard-part map, and their consistency must be checked.

Finally, numerical evidence cannot establish the infinitesimal property. Every floating-point $e>0$ eventually violates $ne<1$. Computation can audit cardinality identities and affine coefficients, while non-Archimedeanness rests on the order relations defining $\varepsilon$.

\section{Future directions}

The event collection can first be treated abstractly as a Boolean algebra and the probability as an ordered-ring-valued finitely additive content. This exposes bounds, monotonicity, complements, and inclusion--exclusion through a reusable interface.

Larger algebras generated by interval cells and infinitesimal atoms are a second target. Equal mass for every real point neither uniquely determines nor automatically remains compatible with interval masses.

A hyperfinite grid with $N$ atoms of mass $1/N$ may be compared with the cut-based model. A standard-part-like map on a suitable ring of finite surreal values should send finite masses to zero and cofinite masses to one, recovering the ordinary zero--one probability.

A theory of admissible countable sums of nonnegative surreal families is needed before $\sigma$-additivity can be posed precisely. Finally, there is a uniqueness question. Permutation invariance and finite additivity force $P(A)=|A|\varepsilon$ for finite $A$; normalization and complements force $P(A)=1-|A^c|\varepsilon$ for cofinite $A$. Thus the present rule is unique once the singleton mass and the standard finite-additivity axioms are fixed.

\section{Conclusion}

The Conway cut $\varepsilon=\{0\mid1,1/2,1/4,\ldots\}$ is positive and below every dyadic $2^{-n}$. Since $n\le2^n$, every finite multiple $n\varepsilon$ remains below one. This arithmetic fits the finite--cofinite algebra exactly: finite events receive the sum of their point masses, while cofinite events receive one minus the mass of their finite exceptions.

The resulting function is nonnegative, normalized, permutation-invariant, and finitely additive. On $[0,1]$, every point has the same nonzero infinitesimal mass although the whole interval has mass one. The construction deliberately remains within finite--cofinite events and finite sums, separating the established model from the further tasks of enlarging the event domain and defining infinite surreal summation.

